==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=adjectival suffix|:inh|enm:-al<id:adjectival suffix>}}
From {{inh|en|enm|-al}}, from the {{der|en|la|-}} adjective suffix {{m|la|-ālis}}, or French, Middle French and Old French {{m|fr|-el}}, {{m|fr|-al}}. Distant {{dbt|en|-ar|nocap=1}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|-all}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}
* {{l|en|-ial}}
* {{l|en|-ual}}
* {{l|en|-arial}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/əl/|[l̩]|[ɫ̩]}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl--al.wav|a=Southern England}}

====Suffix====
{{en-adj}}<br>
{{en-noun|s}}

# {{sid|en|adjectival}} Of or pertaining to. {{ng|Adjectival suffix appended to various words, often nouns, to produce an adjective form. Often added to words of Latin origin, but used with other words also.}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|base|basal}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|cranium|cranial}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|spine|spinal}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|distant|distal}}
# {{sid|en|nominal}} {{ng|Forming nouns, especially of verbal action.}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|propose|proposal}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|deny|denial}}
#: {{suffixusex|en|bestow|bestowal}}

=====Usage notes=====
Most adjectives with this ending were not formed in English, but were taken from Latin (or French). In Latin, the ending {{m|la|-ālis}} (whence {{m+|en|-al}}) had the alternative form {{m|la|-āris}} (whence {{m+|en|-ar}}), which was often used when the base word contained ''l''. The use of ''-al'' or ''-ar'' is inconsistent:
* While words like {{m|en|formular}}, {{m|en|fibular}}, {{m|en|capsular}}, and {{m|en|bulbar}} follow the Latin norm, words like {{m|en|molal}}, {{m|en|phylal}}, {{m|en|salmonellal}}, and {{m|en|zooxanthellal}} do not.
* Some words show variation between {{m|en||-al}} and {{m|en||-ar}}, such as {{m|en|lobar}} and {{m|en|lobal}}, {{m|en|acinar}} and {{m|en|acinal}}; variation also existed in Latin, as with {{m+|la|līneāris}} and hence {{m+|en|linear}} vs {{m+|la|līneālis}} and hence {{m+|en|lineal}}.
However, the ending {{m|en||-al}} is always used if the last ''l'' in the base word is followed by ''r'', e.g. {{m|en|cultural}}, {{m|en|scleral}}, {{m|en|microfloral}}, {{m|en|pleural}}, {{m|en|loral}}, {{m|en|labral}} (compare {{m|en|glomerular}}, where the last ''l'' in {{m|en|glomerulus}} is not followed by ''r''). The ending also appears in the extended forms {{m|en|-ial}} and {{m|en|-ual}}, as in {{m|en|manorial}} and {{m|en|eventual}}.

If the base word ends:
* with {{m|la|-us|us, um or a}}, it is dropped (e.g. {{m|en|viral}}, from {{m|en|virus}}; {{m|en|bronchial}} ({{cog|la-new|bronchiālis}}), from {{m|en|bronchium}}; {{m|en|corneal}} from {{m|en|cornea}});
* with ''er'' / ''re'' (mostly derived from the Latin ending ''-rum''), the ''e'' is dropped (e.g. {{m|en|mem'''br'''al}}, from {{m|en|mem'''ber'''}} ({{noncog|la|memb'''rum'''}}); {{m|en|cen'''tr'''al}}, from {{m|en|center|cen'''ter'''/cen'''tre'''}} ({{noncog|la|cent'''rum'''}}));  or
* with a consonant + ''le'' (mostly derived from the Latin endings {{m|la|-ulus|-ulus, -ulum, -ula}}), the latter usually changes to ''ul'' (e.g. {{m|en|musc'''ul'''ar}}, from {{m|en|musc'''le'''}} ({{noncog|la|mūsc'''ulus'''}}); {{m|en|vehic'''ul'''ar}}, from {{m|en|vehic'''le'''}} ({{noncog|la|vehic'''ulum'''}}); {{m|en|arbusc'''ul'''ar}}, from {{m|en|arbusc'''le'''}} ({{noncog|la|arbusc'''ula'''}})).

As a nominalizer, some verbs have two corresponding nouns, one ending in {{m|en|-al}} and the other in {{m|en|-tion}}/{{m|en|-sion}} (more common suffix), with one or the other being more common, sometimes with different nuances. Notable examples: {{m|en|disposition}}/{{m|en|disposal}} ({{m|en|dispose}}), {{m|en|proposition}}/{{m|en|proposal}} ({{m|en|propose}}), {{m|en|submission}}/{{m|en|submittal}} ({{m|en|submit}}), {{m|en|transmission}}/{{m|en|transmittal}} ({{m|en|transmit}}). Some superficial pairs are actually of different origin, notably {{m|en|reversion}}/{{m|en|reversal}} (respectively related to {{m|en|revert}} and {{m|en|reverse}}).

Final silent {{orth|e}} is dropped before this suffix, e.g. {{m|en|casal}}, from {{m|en|case}}.

